            "text": "Exactly right — and your reasoning is the whole answer.\n\nThere are 4 tiles total: 1 square, 3 round. Every tile is equally likely to be drawn, so:\n\n- P(round) = 3/4 = 75%\n- P(square) = 1/4 = 25%\n\nThe order you dropped them in doesn't matter — a random draw only cares how many of each shape are in the bag at the moment you reach in.\n\nOne thing worth checking, because it's where this usually goes wrong: does the *number* of round tiles change how likely round is, or does it just fill up space?\n\n```openui lifecycle=ephemeral id=tile-draw-probability-check\n\nroot = LearningSurface([check], \"Check the reasoning\", \"One question on what actually drives the probability.\", \"ephemeral\")\ncheck = Question([{ header: \"Why?\", question: \"Suppose the bag had 1 square and 9 round tiles instead. Compared to 3 round tiles, what would change about your chance of drawing a round tile?\", type: \"choice\", choices: [\"It would go up, because more of the 10 tiles are round\", \"It would stay the same, because there is still exactly 1 square in the bag\", \"It would go down, because the bag is more crowded\"], allowText: true, hint: \"Consider the fraction, not just the count.\" }], \"ephemeral\", \"Probability of a random draw\", \"Pick the option you think is right, or explain your own.\")\n\n```"
          }
